BLOCK|h1|1|Cluster-lang: Chapter 1 — The Basics
BLOCK|p||Welcome to Cluster-lang! Cluster is a high-performance systems and scripting language with Pythonic readability, C++ execution speed, and compile-time memory safety.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|1. Variables & Declarations
BLOCK|p||In Cluster-lang, variables can be defined with implicit or explicit type declarations.
BLOCK|h3|3|Implicit Variable Declaration (`:=`)
BLOCK|p||By default, variables are declared and initialized using the `:=` operator. The compiler automatically infers the correct type:
BLOCK|code|python|x := 42                // Inferred as int
name := "Alice"        // Inferred as string
is_valid := True       // Inferred as bool
pi := 3.1415           // Inferred as float
BLOCK|h3|3|Explicit Variable Declaration (`: type :=`)
BLOCK|p||If you want to enforce a specific type or help the compiler's static analysis, add a colon `:` followed by the type name:
BLOCK|code|python|age: int := 25
price: float := 19.99
greeting: string := "Hello, World!"
BLOCK|h3|3|Constants (`constexpr` / `const`)
BLOCK|p||Constants are variables whose values cannot change after initialization. Prefix the declaration with `constexpr` or `const`:
BLOCK|code|python|constexpr PI := 3.14159
constexpr MaxConnections: int := 100
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|2. Basic Data Types
BLOCK|p||Cluster-lang supports the following primitive types:
BLOCK|p||| Type | Description | C++ Under the Hood |
BLOCK|p||| :--- | :--- | :--- |
BLOCK|p||| `int` | Signed 64-bit integer | `int64_t` / `int` |
BLOCK|p||| `float` | 64-bit double-precision floating point | `double` / `float` |
BLOCK|p||| `string` | Text string | `std::string` |
BLOCK|p||| `bool` | Boolean (`True` or `False`) | `bool` |
BLOCK|p||| `void` | Empty/return-free type | `void` |
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|3. Basic Input / Output (`put`)
BLOCK|p||The `put` statement outputs values to the console, automatically appending a newline. You can pass variables, literals, or expressions:
BLOCK|code|python|put "Hello, Alice!"
put 42 + 8
BLOCK|p||To concatenate strings and print them:
BLOCK|code|python|name := "Bob"
put "Hello, " + name
BLOCK|hr||
